# Test: a config under the root of a plugin that never declared is refused, naming that plugin
#
# Question: when the config holds a block under an external plugin's root and
# the plugin cannot be run, does `ze start` refuse with the unknown keyword AND
# say which declared plugin gave ze no config schema, under the doctor code
# for it, with the command that fixes it?
# Why not a unit test: TestEarlyStage1KeepsFailureOutcome proves the outcome is
# kept, and TestReportUndeclaredSchemaRaisesTheWarning that it is reported
# under the code. This proves the outcome of a real fork that fails reaches
# the refusal the operator reads on the terminal.
#
# The run line names a program that does not exist, so the plugin never
# reaches its Stage 1.
#
# Where the code is read: the daemon does not run after this refusal, so the
# code is on the terminal and in the log, on the line boot writes when it
# learns the plugin sent no declaration. `ze show doctor` reports it only on a
# daemon whose config holds no section of the plugin, which then starts.
#
# VALIDATES: AC-8 of spec-plugin-external-yang-at-startup, and the
#   doctor-plugin-schema-undeclared code of AC-14.
# PREVENTS: a bare "unknown top-level keyword: acme-monitor" with no word about
#   the plugin that should have brought the root.

tmpfs=ze.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
bus {
	endpoint absent-plugin {
		external {
			run ./no-such-plugin-program;
		}
		encoding json;
	}
}

acme-monitor {
	target probe-a
	interval 5
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze start ze.conf:timeout=30s
expect=exit:code=1
expect=stderr:contains=unknown top-level keyword
expect=stderr:contains=acme-monitor
expect=stderr:contains=doctor-plugin-schema-undeclared
expect=stderr:contains=a config section that only plugin "absent-plugin" defines is an unknown keyword to ze
expect=stderr:contains=external plugin "absent-plugin" could not be started, so it declared nothing
expect=stderr:contains=fix: ze show plugin conformance run "./no-such-plugin-program"
reject=stderr:contains=Ze running.
